.TH IPPFIX 8 "August 2026" "1.0" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
ippfix \- IPP proxy that repairs print jobs for printers with a limited font
cache.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ippfix
[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-advertise\fR \fIADDRESS\fR]
[\fB\-\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-converter\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-advertise\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-convert\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
\fI[NAME=]URI\fR...

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ippfix
accepts print jobs over IPP, rewrites each PDF so that it contains no embedded
font programs, and forwards it to a real printer. Everything else is relayed
untouched.
.PP
It exists because HP LaserJet Pro printers run a combined PostScript/PDF
interpreter with a fixed per\-page budget for embedded fonts and the glyphs
drawn from them. Exceeding that budget aborts the interpreter: the printer
warms up, reports the job
.BR completed ,
and marks nothing. Sometimes the first few pages emerge and the job then stops.
No error reaches the client and none is shown on the panel. Where the device
records it at all, its event log reports an assertion failure in
.BR fontcache.c .
.PP
The budget covers both the glyphs drawn and the embedded font programs they
come from, and the two trade against each other. On a Color LaserJet Pro MFP
M283fdw, one fully embedded font renders 527 distinct glyphs but not 534; add a
second fully embedded font and the page fails at 300. A font's cost scales with
how many glyphs its embedded program declares rather than being a flat
per\-font constant, so a heavily subsetted face is far cheaper than a complete
one.
.PP
Those figures come from probes embedding complete, unsubsetted fonts. Jobs from
a browser subset aggressively and sit well below them: sampled from real Chrome
output, two subsets declaring 93 and 668 glyphs, with 451 distinct glyphs drawn
between them, printed without trouble. No fixed threshold is therefore safe to
design against \(em whether a document crosses the line depends on its
typefaces, how they were subsetted, and how many distinct characters appear.
.PP
The defect appears in firmware builds years apart and is unlikely to be fixed.
Client\-side changes affect how often it is reached: Chrome 130 through 144
embedded a separate font program for every
.I strike
of a typeface, including one per text colour, which multiplied the cost of an
ordinary page. Chrome 145 removed the colour component and normalises text size
out of the key, so current versions embed far fewer font programs than that era
did.
.PP
.B ippfix
converts glyphs to filled paths using Ghostscript's
.B \-dNoOutputFonts
option. No font program reaches the printer, which makes the failure
structurally impossible rather than merely less likely. Text remains vector, so
the printer's own rasteriser still renders it at full device precision and its
edge enhancement still applies. Rasterising the page instead would commit its
geometry to the device grid before the printer saw it, forfeit the printer's
halftoning, and inflate a small job into tens of megabytes.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I [NAME=]URI
A printer to proxy, given as an
.B ipp://
or
.B ipps://
URI. The optional
.I NAME
becomes the queue name, published at
.BI /ipp/ NAME\fR.
Without one, the queue is named
.BR print .
May be repeated to serve several printers from one daemon.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-advertise " \fIADDRESS\fR"
Address that clients should use to reach this server. Autodetected by default,
which is wrong on hosts with several addresses.
.TP
.B \-\-cert " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS certificate. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt .
.TP
.B \-\-converter " \fIPATH\fR"
Helper that rewrites a PDF read on standard input and writes the result to
standard output. Default
.IR /usr/local/lib/ippfix/defont .
.TP
.B \-\-key " \fIFILE\fR"
TLS private key. Default
.IR /etc/ippfix/ippfix.key .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-advertise
Do not publish the queues over DNS\-SD. Useful when discovery is handled
elsewhere.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-convert
Relay jobs untouched. Intended for comparison against a converting instance,
to confirm that conversion is what makes the difference.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " \fIPORT\fR"
Port to listen on. Default 631, which requires
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
or an adjusted
.IR net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start .
.TP
.B \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
Time allowed for each conversion and each request to the printer. Default 300.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log protocol detail.

.SH BEHAVIOUR
Both plaintext IPP and implicit TLS are accepted on the same port, and both
IPv4 and IPv6 are served.
.PP
Printer attributes are mirrored from the real device on every request. Only
identity and URIs are rewritten \(em the queue name, its UUID, the URIs clients
should use, and the locations of the printer's icons and localised strings,
which are re\-served by this daemon because clients may have no route to the
printer's own web server. Capabilities and status pass through unchanged, so
duplex, trays, media, colour modes and live supply and error state are always
correct without configuration.
.PP
Documents that are not PDF are relayed untouched, as are PDFs whose conversion
fails or leaves any font program behind. A job that might not print is
preferable to one that prints something wrong.
.PP
Jobs are forwarded one at a time, because affected printers report
.B multiple\-document\-jobs\-supported
as false and mishandle a second job arriving mid\-transfer.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ippfix/
Installation directory.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ippfix/defont
PDF conversion helper. May be run by hand for diagnosis.
.TP
.I /etc/ippfix/ippfix.crt\fR, \fI/etc/ippfix/ippfix.key
Self\-signed TLS credentials created by the installer.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/ippfix.service
Service unit; edit
.B ExecStart
to configure the printers.

.SH EXAMPLES
Serve one printer:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Serve two, under explicit names:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix upstairs=ipp://printer1.example/ipp/print \\
       downstairs=ipp://printer2.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a second, non\-converting instance for comparison:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ippfix \-\-port 6310 \-\-no\-advertise \-\-no\-convert \\
       ipp://printer.example/ipp/print
.fi
.RE

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each job is logged with its queue, operation, and the result of conversion:
.PP
.RS
.nf
upstairs   Print\-Job   HTTP 200  [outlined 393836 \-> 76789 bytes in 0.2s]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B relayed
indicates the job was passed through unchanged.
.PP
Because an affected printer reports success whether or not it printed, the
reliable check is the device's own total impression count, which does not
advance for a job that died. On HP devices both that counter and the assertion
log are readable over HTTP from
.I /DevMgmt/ProductUsageDyn.xml
and
.IR /DevMgmt/ProductLogsDyn.xml .

.SH NOTES
If clients can also reach the printers directly, they will see both those and
the proxy queues, and users will choose arbitrarily between them. Suppress the
printers' own
.BR _ipp ,
.B _ipps
and
.B _pdl\-datastream
advertisements, but leave
.B _uscan
and
.B _uscans
alone or scanning will stop working.
.PP
Only one mDNS responder may hold UDP port 5353. With
.B systemd\-resolved
present, set
.B MulticastDNS=no
in
.I /etc/systemd/resolved.conf
and restart it, or run with
.BR \-\-no\-advertise .

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gs (1),
.BR cups (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)

